    {
      "id": "claim_fb1609461b7906c99582736e3ca7a8a5bfbedf98ec25a1255057b3c85d071ea1",
      "kind": "claim",
      "title": "Poincare-Bendixson Theorem",
      "metadata_json": {
        "artifact_digest": "sha256:5cec1839415c31a74b268126b940fd0dd0024252898ef372eb7adf8eaf83abd1",
        "artifact_path": "objects/sha256/5c/5cec1839415c31a74b268126b940fd0dd0024252898ef372eb7adf8eaf83abd1.md",
        "bundle_digest": "sha256:13502a6f604092f913abdc5ca02888f14fb70021562edde363f87543f45de51d",
        "bundle_entry_key": "thm-poincare-bendixson",
        "bundle_id": "population.geometry-dynamics-pde-001",
        "claim_kind": "theorem",
        "conditions": [],
        "domain": "dynamical systems / planar ODE",
        "draft": "claude-8c0397aa/bundle_g01.json",
        "evidence_ids": [],
        "logical_id": "math:dynamics.ode:poincare-bendixson-theorem",
        "overlay_schema_version": 1,
        "proof_text": "The omega-limit set of a bounded forward orbit is nonempty, compact, connected, and invariant under the flow, by standard limit-set facts using compactness and continuity of the flow. Pick q in omega(gamma^+); by invariance, the whole orbit through q lies in omega(gamma^+). Construct a short line segment Sigma through a non-equilibrium point of that orbit, transverse to the vector field (possible since omega(gamma^+) has no equilibria); the flow is then transverse to Sigma nearby, and successive intersections of the orbit through q with Sigma are monotonic along Sigma, a planar fact following from the Jordan curve theorem applied to the closed curve formed by an arc of the orbit between two consecutive intersections together with the segment of Sigma joining them, which separates the plane so the rest of the trajectory cannot cross it. A monotone bounded sequence of intersection points on Sigma converges; if the limit were not the starting intersection point itself, one derives a contradiction using that gamma^+ accumulates on all of omega(gamma^+), so the orbit through q must return exactly to its starting point on Sigma, i.e. be periodic. Finally gamma^+ itself is shown to spiral onto this periodic orbit, so omega(gamma^+) equals it.",
        "release_manifest_digest": "sha256:c1fd189475f9669f6e1d375b115cb02df9ccf19cb173abf439ef25d92982d191",
        "revision_digest": "sha256:fb1609461b7906c99582736e3ca7a8a5bfbedf98ec25a1255057b3c85d071ea1",
        "source_id": "wikipedia:Poincare-Bendixson theorem",
        "source_revision": "as-read-2026-08-02",
        "statement_fidelity_reviewed": false,
        "supersedes": [],
        "trust_class": "curated_secondary",
        "truth_status": "unverified"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_61de81af8602acea60edffe88c99fef283c25146015cae1336357d3d7e915f9b",
      "kind": "claim",
      "title": "Poincare Recurrence Theorem",
      "metadata_json": {
        "artifact_digest": "sha256:b4a09868cc274424a42af4da9eec6391aad1aad9e07bf1004b7f78f54cd3e434",
        "artifact_path": "objects/sha256/b4/b4a09868cc274424a42af4da9eec6391aad1aad9e07bf1004b7f78f54cd3e434.md",
        "bundle_digest": "sha256:13502a6f604092f913abdc5ca02888f14fb70021562edde363f87543f45de51d",
        "bundle_entry_key": "thm-poincare-recurrence",
        "bundle_id": "population.geometry-dynamics-pde-001",
        "claim_kind": "theorem",
        "conditions": [],
        "domain": "ergodic theory",
        "draft": "claude-8c0397aa/bundle_g01.json",
        "evidence_ids": [],
        "logical_id": "math:dynamics.ergodic:poincare-recurrence-theorem",
        "overlay_schema_version": 1,
        "proof_text": "It suffices to show the set of points of A that return to A only finitely often has measure zero. Let B = {x in A : T^n(x) not in A for all n>=1}, the points of A that never return; B is measurable, and B, T^{-1}B, T^{-2}B,... are pairwise disjoint (if T^{-i}B and T^{-j}B, i<j, shared a point x, then T^i(x) is in B but T^{j-i}(T^i(x))=T^j(x) is also in B subset A, contradicting that T^i(x) never returns to A), each with measure mu(B) by measure preservation. Since mu(X) is finite, an infinite disjoint union of sets of equal positive measure would have infinite total measure, so mu(B)=0. Applying the same disjointness argument to the (countable union of) sets of points that return only finitely many times, built from B-type sets along the orbit, shows that set also has measure zero, giving the stated almost-everywhere infinite recurrence.",
        "release_manifest_digest": "sha256:c1fd189475f9669f6e1d375b115cb02df9ccf19cb173abf439ef25d92982d191",
        "revision_digest": "sha256:61de81af8602acea60edffe88c99fef283c25146015cae1336357d3d7e915f9b",
        "source_id": "wikipedia:Poincare recurrence theorem",
        "source_revision": "as-read-2026-08-02",
        "statement_fidelity_reviewed": false,
        "supersedes": [],
        "trust_class": "curated_secondary",
        "truth_status": "unverified"
      }
    },
